<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Tambora</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1815 CE (approx.) · capital Tambora</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small kingdom on the slopes of Mount Tambora in Sumbawa, wiped out by the largest volcanic eruption in recorded history.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Little is known of its origins. Tambora was one of several small realms on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumbawa">Sumbawa</a>, alongside the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bima-sultanate">Bima Sultanate</a>, Dompu, Sanggar and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sumbawa-sultanate">Sumbawa Sultanate</a>, and from the 17th century it came under Makassarese and then <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a> influence through treaties with Sumbawan rulers.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its people grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and traded horses, honey, sappanwood and sandalwood. Tambora was ruled by a Muslim raja. Its language, recorded in a short wordlist gathered for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stamford-raffles">Thomas Stamford Raffles</a>, appears unrelated to the Austronesian languages around it and may have been a non-Austronesian isolate.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The eruption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-tambora">Mount Tambora</a> in April 1815 buried the kingdom and neighbouring Pekat under ash and pyroclastic flows, killing nearly all its inhabitants. The ensuing global cooling caused the Year Without a Summer in 1816. Excavations since 2004 have uncovered houses, pottery and remains, earning Tambora the name the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompeii">Pompeii</a> of the East.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1600 CE – 1815 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Maritime Southeast Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:91.54%;width:4.28%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1815 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tambora</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tambora</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tambora</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1708&amp;focus=kingdom-of-tambora" aria-label="Kingdom of Tambora on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7941 2486 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Tambora lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8261 2742h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8261 2742h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tambora. <a href="/atlas?year=1708&amp;focus=kingdom-of-tambora">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

